SLUG: faq.html
TITLE: Packers & Movers FAQ — Kanpur shifting questions answered
DESC: Straight answers on booking notice, how moving costs are worked out in Kanpur, packing, damage and claims, car transport, storage, and buildings with no lift.
HEADING: Frequently asked questions
LEAD: The questions we are actually asked, answered properly. If yours is not here, ask us — we will add it.
CRUMBS: Home|/ ; FAQ|
---

  <section class="section on-paper">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="faq" data-faq>

        <details open>
          <summary>How far in advance should I book a move?</summary>
          <div class="faq__body"><p>
            For a local move, one to two weeks is comfortable. For an intercity
            move, two to three weeks gives time to survey, confirm a vehicle
            and schedule a crew. We do take shorter-notice work when a vehicle
            is free, so it is always worth asking — but the last few days of
            the month book out first.
          </p></div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>How is the cost of a move calculated?</summary>
          <div class="faq__body"><p>
            Four things drive it: how much there is to move, how far it is
            going, how much packing you want us to do, and how hard the access
            is at each end — floor, lift and how far the vehicle can park from
            the door. We survey first and quote in writing with those parts
            itemised, so you can see which one is costing you money.
          </p></div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Do you pack everything, or should I pack some of it myself?</summary>
          <div class="faq__body"><p>
            Either works. The most common arrangement is that we pack the
            kitchen, glassware and electronics, and you handle clothes, books
            and linen yourself. That brings both the material and the labour
            cost down without putting the breakable things at risk. Tell us
            which rooms you want to do and we will scope around it.
          </p></div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>What happens if something is damaged during the move?</summary>
          <div class="faq__body"><p>
            Raise it with your coordinator at delivery, while the crew is still
            on site, and note it on the delivery sheet. That is what makes a
            claim straightforward later. The cover itself depends on the
            transit-insurance option you choose, which we confirm in writing
            with your quote before the move — ask for that detail rather than
            assuming goods are automatically insured, with us or with anyone.
          </p></div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Can you move my car or bike as well?</summary>
          <div class="faq__body"><p>
            Yes. Vehicles travel in a covered carrier rather than being driven,
            so no distance is added to the odometer. We need the registration
            papers and the tank at roughly a quarter full. It can run on the
            same booking as your household goods, and the vehicle's condition
            is photographed at both ends.
          </p></div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>What if my new home is not ready on the delivery date?</summary>
          <div class="faq__body"><p>
            Tell us as early as you can and we will hold the consignment in
            storage and deliver when you are ready. Storage is charged
            separately from the move, by duration, and we confirm that rate
            before anything goes into the facility. Arranging it in advance
            always costs less than arranging it on the day.
          </p></div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>My building has no lift and narrow stairs. Is that a problem?</summary>
          <div class="faq__body"><p>
            Not if we know in advance. What causes delays and extra cost is
            finding out on the day. During the survey we check the floor, the
            lift size, the stair width and how far the vehicle can park from
            the door, then plan the crew size and equipment around it. A fourth
            floor without a lift needs more people, not more hours.
          </p></div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Do you work on Sundays and public holidays?</summary>
          <div class="faq__body"><p>
            Yes. For office relocations a weekend is usually the better choice,
            because it gives two clear days to move and reconnect everything
            before staff return. Weekend slots fill earlier, so book ahead.
          </p></div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Can you move plants, pets, or a gas cylinder?</summary>
          <div class="faq__body"><p>
            Plants travel badly in a sealed vehicle over long distances and we
            will say so rather than take the money; for a local move they are
            usually fine. Pets should travel with you, not in the goods
            vehicle. Gas cylinders cannot be carried loaded — have the cylinder
            emptied and the connection removed by the supplier before the day.
          </p></div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Do I need to be there on the day?</summary>
          <div class="faq__body"><p>
            Someone should be, at both ends, to sign the inventory and to say
            where things go. It does not have to be you — but it should be
            somebody who can make decisions, because "leave it, they will
            know" is how boxes end up in the wrong room.
          </p></div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>How much notice do you need to cancel or reschedule?</summary>
          <div class="faq__body"><p>
            Tell us as soon as you know. A date moved with several days'
            notice is usually straightforward. Cancellation terms, including
            any charge once a vehicle and crew have been committed, are set
            out with your written quote — read that section before you accept,
            with us or with any mover.
          </p></div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Will one vehicle be enough for my things?</summary>
          <div class="faq__body"><p>
            That is exactly what the survey settles. Under-sizing the vehicle
            is the classic way a cheap quote becomes an expensive day, because
            a second trip is charged on top. We size the vehicle against the
            inventory and tell you which one we are sending.
          </p></div>
        </details>

      </div>

      <p class="mt-7">
        <a class="link-cta" href="/contact.html">
          Still have a question? Ask us
          <svg class="ic" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-arrow-right"/></svg>
        </a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section is-tight cta-band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="wrap-narrow text-centre">
        <h2>Get a straight answer and a written quote</h2>
        <p class="lead">Two minutes of detail is usually enough for us to scope the job.</p>
        <div class="btn-row mt-6 btn-row--centre">
          <a class="btn btn--primary btn--lg" href="/contact.html" data-open-callback>Request a callback</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
